{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792791235,"nonce":"Pje9xDgThK+kIIGvx9EIOHmHMtc=","title":"TypeScript: switch, do-while and labeled jumps","message":"switch statements dispatch with gotos to clause labels in source order, so clauses fall through; case tests are lowered in order with strict equality and must be comparable with the discriminant (literal types of templates included). Enum discriminants are narrowed per clause and past every case for default; a switch whose cases cover every enum member makes the end of a returning function unreachable, and a number outside the enum then traps. do/while lowers to while (first || test) so continue still evaluates the test; while, for and do exits narrow by the negated test when no break leaves. Labeled break and continue use the innermost loop's native jump when they target it and gotos otherwise; labeled blocks take break. Definite assignment merges the flow at every switch exit. Gaps: clauses sharing block-scoped declarations and switches over unions report Unsupported. The shared CFG drops labels after a return in one statement list (932b983); clauses and labeled loop bodies are nested blocks to avoid it. Official corpus: PASS 948 -\u003e 960, FAIL 103, UNSUPPORTED 2353 -\u003e 2341, SKIP 3421, no regressions. Parent: ad01ba90511bf73c217ac689d93556c94e0de3e83bd990db4057e8fd89174827.","files":null}]}